    insight: '88.5% of UAE residents are expatriates and South Asians are roughly 62% of them. Malayalam has around 1.06m speakers here, Tamil 455,000. First Day First Show is a real, deeply-held ritual — opening day, first show, whistles, cutouts, the whole thing. Every UAE chain markets a generic English-language luxury proposition to a country whose largest film audience celebrates cinema more intensely than any other group in it. Nobody is speaking to them. Cinemacity, with Prime Pictures behind it, is better placed to than anyone.',
    territory: 'Loud, warm, communal, genuinely fan-first. Not a brand observing a culture from outside — a brand that books the room and turns up. Language-native where it matters, never subtitled as an afterthought.',
    includes: 'Opening-night crowd energy. Language-specific release calendars as a genuine service. Fan community collaboration. Regional star moments. The Arabic and Filipino equivalents of the same ritual. Cinema Akil-adjacent arthouse for the film-culture audience.',
    formats: [
      { name: 'The Countdown', desc: 'A recurring release-week format built around the specific title and the specific room, in the language of its audience. Not a trailer repost — an invitation to a night.' },
      { name: 'First Show Energy', desc: 'The queue, the whistles, the applause, the moment the lights drop. Captured on opening night, published within hours. Documentary, not produced.' },
      { name: 'What’s Releasing In Your Language', desc: 'A monthly service post — Malayalam, Tamil, Hindi, Telugu, Arabic, Tagalog releases with dates and rooms. Nobody else publishes this. It will be saved and shared constantly.' }
    ],
    executions: [
      'Reel: the last sixty seconds before a first-day-first-show, no commentary, just the room filling and the sound rising.',
      'TikTok: a fan community leader introducing the release in Malayalam, filmed in the lobby, captioned in English.',
      'Carousel: the month’s South Indian releases with dates, rooms and booking links — pure utility.',
      'Story takeover: handing the account to a fan community on a major release night.'
    ],
    production: 'Handheld, real light, real sound, minimal grade. This pillar must never look like it was art-directed. The only brand device is the frame edge and the room name.',
    platforms: 'TikTok and Instagram Reels lead. Facebook for family and community reach. Stories for the live moments.',
    measurement: 'Reach among non-followers · shares · opening-weekend booking share for the relevant titles · saves on calendar posts.',
    flag: 'Requires client confirmation: which language titles Cinemacity actually programmes, and the release slate from Prime Pictures. This pillar is only as good as the programming behind it.',
    videos: [
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Reel · 9:16', platform: 'Instagram Reels', credit: 'OPTIONAL_CREATOR_NAME', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' },
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'TikTok · 9:16', platform: 'TikTok', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'late-show',
    n: '03',
    name: 'The Late Show',
    line: 'The night, not the film.',
    role: 'Occasion. The pillar that shows Cinemacity as an evening rather than a screening — and the one that makes the venues themselves the content.',
    insight: 'TikTok’s own research finds 72% of moviegoers attend with others. The decision is a negotiation about an evening, not a solo choice about a film. Cinemacity is the only UAE chain that can serve a drink in the auditorium, and it has Azul, 1927, Graffiti, Backlot and a 500-seat live-events theatre. Its real competitor on a Thursday night is not VOX — it is a restaurant.',
    territory: 'Low light, late hours, adult. Confident rather than loud. This is the most premium-feeling pillar and the one that should look least like cinema marketing.',
    includes: 'Azul and 1927. Licensed screenings. Starlight live programming — music, theatre, events. Live sport, building on Matchday, Together. Graffiti and Backlot. Late sessions. Private hire including Theatre FIVE.',
    formats: [
      { name: 'Double Feature', desc: 'A film paired with what to eat and drink around it, at a specific venue. Two halves of one night. Runs weekly and directly serves F&B attach.' },
      { name: 'After The Credits', desc: 'What the room becomes once the film ends — Azul at midnight, the lounge, the bar. The part of the night nobody else can show.' },
      { name: 'Matchday', desc: 'The established live-sport property, extended. Cricket is the largest opportunity in the window given the audience.' }
    ],
    executions: [
      'Reel: Azul at 11pm — no dialogue, no titles until the last frame. Sold entirely on light and sound.',
      'Instagram carousel: the Thursday night plan at Fountain Views, valet to last call, with times.',
      'TikTok: what the 1927 members’ club actually is, filmed honestly — scarcity works when it is not oversold.',
      'Story: a live-sport fixture with a booking sticker and the licensed-venue detail stated plainly.'
    ],
    production: 'Available light, deep shadow, warm highlights. Shallow depth of field. Music-led with real ambience underneath. Type minimal and small — this pillar earns its premium by restraint.',
    platforms: 'Instagram lead. Facebook Events for Starlight programming. TikTok for the honest, unpolished version.',
    measurement: 'Saves and shares on occasion content · event RSVPs and Facebook Event responses · licensed-venue page sessions from social. F&B attach on social-sourced bookings is the ideal read and depends on client data.',
    videos: [
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Reel · 9:16', platform: 'Instagram Reels', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' },
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Reel · 9:16', platform: 'Instagram Reels', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' },
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Event film · 16:9', platform: 'Facebook / YouTube', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'second-weekend',
    n: '04',
    name: 'Second Weekend',
    line: 'The window the distributor abandons.',
    role: 'Sustain and advocacy. Extends the commercial life of every title after the marketing spend stops.',
    insight: 'Distributors spend everything on opening weekend and go quiet. But 90.7% of viewers attend within thirty days, and research from TikTok, Cinema United and Comscore shows that fan conversation after opening weekend is precisely what flattens the second-weekend drop. Warner Bros reports creator content outperforming traditional advertising three to one. The exhibitor is the only party with a commercial interest in weekends two, three and four — and in this market nobody is working them.',
    territory: 'Conversational, spoiler-aware, opinionated. The pillar where Cinemacity talks about films like a person rather than a listings page. It requires a point of view and the confidence to have one.',
    includes: 'Post-credits and ending discussion in declared spoiler space. Audience verdicts captured on opening night and published on day four. Still-showing reminders tied to specific rooms. Re-release and repertory programming. Awards-season sustain.',
    formats: [
      { name: 'We Need To Talk About That Ending', desc: 'Clearly-flagged spoiler content published on day four, when the audience wants it and the distributor has stopped. The format the category is missing entirely.' },
      { name: 'Verdict', desc: 'Audience reactions captured in the lobby on opening night, cut and published mid-week as social proof for late deciders. Replaces generic vox pops with a job to do.' },
      { name: 'Still Showing', desc: 'A weekly reminder of what is still on, in which room, with a session link. Unglamorous, and it will drive bookings.' }
    ],
    executions: [
      'Reel: a two-minute breakdown of a post-credits scene, spoiler card on frame one, published Monday.',
      'TikTok: “We asked 40 people leaving the 9pm show to rate it out of 10” — fast cuts, real answers, published Tuesday.',
      'Carousel: still showing this week, one slide per title, room named, link in bio pointed at the session.',
      'Story: a Thursday poll on what to rewatch, with the answer bookable in the same tap.'
    ],
    production: 'Editorial and fast. Screen-recording and text-led formats are welcome here — this pillar should feel cheap to make and hard to ignore. Consistent spoiler-warning device.',
    platforms: 'Instagram and TikTok equally. This is the pillar most likely to generate genuine comment threads.',
    measurement: 'Comment volume and sentiment · shares · link taps on Still Showing · reach in weeks two and three versus week one. Week-by-week booking share would confirm it and requires client data.',
    videos: [
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Reel · 9:16', platform: 'Instagram Reels', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' },
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'TikTok · 9:16', platform: 'TikTok', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'house-rules',
    n: '05',
    name: 'House Rules',
    line: 'The only thing everyone in a UAE cinema agrees on.',
    role: 'Always-on brand personality. Keeps the account alive in empty release weeks and defends the theatrical experience against the sofa.',
    insight: 'In July, the account’s single best-performing asset ever was an original pop-culture meme that reached two million views — far beyond anything a distributor asset has delivered. Entertainment content works. What it lacks is a point of view. The shared code of moviegoing — phones, talkers, latecomers, seat-kickers, the person who explains the plot — is the one cultural reference every one of the UAE’s two hundred nationalities holds in common. It is funny, endlessly renewable, and it lets Cinemacity have an opinion instead of a meme feed.',
    territory: 'Dry, deadpan, institutional. Cinemacity as the house that has been doing this for eighty years and has views. Numbered rules, delivered straight. Never punching at the audience — always on the side of the people who came to watch the film.',
    includes: 'The numbered House Rules series. Staff and usher perspectives. Cinema etiquette. The case for the big screen over the sofa. Reactive cultural moments, filtered through the same voice.',
    formats: [
      { name: 'House Rule No. __', desc: 'A numbered, escalating series. Each rule a single card or a six-second film. Instantly recognisable, infinitely extendable, and it builds a body of work rather than a stream of posts.' },
      { name: 'Reported By Our Ushers', desc: 'Real things that happen in cinemas, told by the people who see them. Uses staff as content assets and gives the brand warmth.' },
      { name: 'Sofa vs Screen', desc: 'The honest argument for the room, made without begging. The pillar’s commercial job, and the whole positioning in miniature.' }
    ],
    executions: [
      'Static: House Rule No. 14 — set in the deck’s display face on black, no image. Designed to be screenshotted.',
      'Reel: an usher describing the strangest thing found in a screen, straight to camera, no music.',
      'TikTok: reacting to a trending audio in the voice of the house, without naming a title.',
      'Carousel: the full rulebook so far — a recurring, growing artefact people will return to.'
    ],
    production: 'The most typographic pillar. Often no photography at all. When there is, it is real and unlit. This is where the visual system proves it can hold attention with nothing but type and black.',
    platforms: 'Instagram for the static rulebook, TikTok for the performed version. The most shareable pillar in the system.',
    measurement: 'Shares and saves · reach from non-followers · comment volume · share of monthly reach in weeks with no major release.',
    videos: [
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'Reel · 9:16', platform: 'Instagram Reels', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' },
      { title: 'VIDEO_TITLE', description: 'VIDEO_DESCRIPTION', format: 'TikTok · 9:16', platform: 'TikTok', credit: '', thumbnail: 'VIDEO_THUMBNAIL_URL', driveUrl: 'GOOGLE_DRIVE_VIDEO_URL', externalUrl: '' }
